Anyway, I'm on there are JaredC137, so add me as a friend and trade me all your Meeseek swag.

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:44.100
Now, let's get on with the show.

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:50.900
Dan Harmon states that every story – from the Odyssey to your standard fart joke – follows 8 simple steps.

00:01:50.900 --> 00:01:54.710
Harmon called it “the story embryo”, and
it goes like this:

00:01:54.710 --> 00:01:57.700
1. A character is in a zone of comfort.

00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:00.530
2. But they want something.

00:02:00.530 --> 00:02:03.500
3.They enter an unfamiliar situation.

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:05.500
4. Adapt to it.

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:07.700
5. Get what they wanted.

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:10.800
6. Pay a heavy price for it.

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:14.300
7. Then return to their familiar situation.

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:16.200
8. Having changed.

00:02:16.269 --> 00:02:19.799
Of course, this can all change based on the
genre and medium.

00:02:19.799 --> 00:02:21.700
And it especially changes for television.

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:26.430
Whereas a movie’s goal is to send viewers
out on a 90 minute high, television is a bit

00:02:26.430 --> 00:02:29.000
different: it aims to keep people watching
television.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:30.000
Forever.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:35.600
Harmon says this is all to put your brain in a vegetative state so that the commercials can do their work.

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:40.700
This means television shows are less about
change, and more about preserving the status quo.

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:45.930
In the Dark Knight Rises, Batman saves Gotham
before jetting off to Italy to start his new life.

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:50.510
While in Batman: The Animated Series, you
can bet your bottom dollar that each episode

00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:55.180
begins and ends with Batman fighting a different
villain in the same old Gotham.

00:02:55.180 --> 00:03:00.689
In Harmon’s words, television “swaps out any meaningful and therefore potentially television-subverting

00:03:00.689 --> 00:03:05.270
truth with the basic, eternal "truth" that
change is unnecessary” (46).

00:03:05.270 --> 00:03:09.989
With that in mind, the structure of a TV episode
often ends up looking more like this:

00:03:09.989 --> 00:03:12.000
1. "I (the protagonist)

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:14.300
2. Notice a small problem.

00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:16.800
3. And make a major decision.

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:19.000
4. This changes things

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:22.000
5. To some satisfaction, but

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:24.200
6. There are consequences

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:26.300
7. That must be undone

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:29.200
8. And I must admit the futility of change."

00:03:29.879 --> 00:03:33.200
So do Justin Roiland and Dan Harmon follow
their own advice?

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:34.300
Absolutely.

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:38.409
Every episode of Rick and Morty follows this
format, but for the sake of time, let’s

00:03:38.409 --> 00:03:44.000
see how it plays out in one of my favorites:
Season 2, Episode 6: “The Ricks Must Be Crazy”.

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:47.849
Step 1: Establish a protagonist in a zone
of comfort.

00:03:47.849 --> 00:03:51.950
Harmon tells us that it’s best not to “screw
around” here and fade immediately onto your

00:03:51.950 --> 00:03:54.150
characters doing something relatable.

00:03:54.150 --> 00:03:59.100
This episode does just that, opening with Summer, Rick, and Morty coming out of the movie, Ball Fondlers.

00:03:59.189 --> 00:04:03.600
In just 20 short seconds, the episode has told us who our protagonists are,

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:05.800
and what they, and what their world is like.

00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:08.400
There's pros and cons to (burp) every alternate timeline.

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:12.360
Fun facts about this one - its got giant telepathic spiders, eleven 9/11s,

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:15.300
aaaannnnnnddd the BEST ICE CREAM IN THE MULTIVERSE!!!

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:16.000
- Shut up!
- Woah!

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:18.400
And 25 seconds later, the script moves to.

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:22.930
Step 2: Introduce a small problem and establish
a need.

00:04:22.930 --> 00:04:26.870
Step 2 is where the protagonist realizes that
life isn’t perfect.

00:04:26.870 --> 00:04:30.680
In a teenage love story, this is when the
hero wonders why he can’t be the school

00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:32.720
quarterback and date the cheerleader.

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:36.300
In a horror film, this is when the group of
teens first hears that someone was murdered

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:38.139
in the woods 13 years ago.

00:04:38.139 --> 00:04:41.870
For the Sanchez family, though, it’s when
Rick’s ship refuses to start.

00:04:41.870 --> 00:04:43.009
Rick: "Oh great…

00:04:43.009 --> 00:04:45.270
Huh, looks like something’s wrong with the
Microverse Battery.

00:04:45.270 --> 00:04:46.539
We’re going to have to go inside.”

00:04:46.539 --> 00:04:50.300
In two lines, Rick sets up the central problem
and motivation of the script:

00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:53.400
the ship’s battery is dead, and they need to fix it.

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:58.850
In typical hero fashion, Morty is reluctant
to start this journey: “Um, go inside what?”

00:04:58.850 --> 00:05:03.500
This reluctance is an often used narrative
trick that helps us identify with the protagonist,

00:05:03.500 --> 00:05:04.770
in this case Morty.

00:05:04.770 --> 00:05:09.520
Morty’s uncertainty of the situation becomes
our uncertainty, and we find ourselves more

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:11.180
invested in the story.

00:05:11.180 --> 00:05:13.020
Ten seconds later and the script moves us to:

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:18.410
Step 3: The protagonist makes a major decision
and enters an unfamiliar situation.

00:05:18.410 --> 00:05:22.099
Step 3 is where the descent into the unknown begins.

00:05:22.099 --> 00:05:26.760
In the case of Rick and Morty, this descent
is both a figurative and a literal one.

00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:32.700
On one level, the two are leaving their comfort zone and entering one characterized by danger and uncertainty.

00:05:32.759 --> 00:05:37.050
While on a more literal level, they’re actually
shrinking down to visit a world inside

00:05:37.050 --> 00:05:39.330
Rick’s car battery, posing as aliens:

00:05:39.330 --> 00:05:42.020
Rick: “There’s nothing dishonest about
what we’re doing.

00:05:42.020 --> 00:05:43.160
Now slap on these antennas.

00:05:43.160 --> 00:05:44.610
These people need to believe we’re aliens.”

00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:49.090
Harmon says that it’s not important how
big or epic your story is, but rather how

00:05:49.090 --> 00:05:52.310
noticeable the contrast is between your two worlds.

00:05:52.310 --> 00:05:54.800
This episode takes that premise and runs with it,

00:05:54.800 --> 00:05:58.270
introducing us to a world that is completely foreign to our own.

00:05:58.270 --> 00:06:02.610
It’s populated by Martian-like people living
in power plant inspired buildings, who venerate

00:06:02.610 --> 00:06:05.030
Rick for introducing giving them electricity.

00:06:05.030 --> 00:06:09.340
Unknown to them, the Stair Master like Gooble
Boxes Rick gave them actually redirect most

00:06:09.340 --> 00:06:11.130
of their power to Rick’s ship.

00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:16.300
And, like the dick of a God he is, Rick has
guided their civilization in some messed up ways.

00:06:16.300 --> 00:06:19.460
Morty, you gotta flip them off. I told them it means peace among worlds.

00:06:19.460 --> 00:06:20.979
How hilarious is that?

00:06:20.979 --> 00:06:27.070
Which brings us to Step 4: Characters adapt
to the new situation and things must change.

00:06:27.070 --> 00:06:30.620
Step 4 is all about the characters learning
their place in this new world.

00:06:30.620 --> 00:06:36.300
It’s where all of their neuroses are broken down and all their psychological baggage is slowly stripped away.

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:39.000
Movie producers call it the “training phase”.

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:43.599
But remember, TV isn’t about selling change
– it’s about preserving the status quo

00:06:43.599 --> 00:06:45.629
so you’ll tune in week after week.

00:06:45.629 --> 00:06:51.360
As such, this stage is much less about internal
change and more about external change.

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:55.099
Once they arrive in the Microverse, Rick and
Morty orient themselves in this new world

00:06:55.099 --> 00:06:58.229
and identify the problem at hand: Zeep Xanflorp.

00:06:58.229 --> 00:07:03.669
Zeep is perhaps the most familiar and strangest
aspect of this Microverse, essentially a carbon

00:07:03.669 --> 00:07:06.349
copy of a carbon-copy of Rick himself: “I
dropped out of school.

00:07:06.349 --> 00:07:07.969
It’s not a place for smart people.”

00:07:07.969 --> 00:07:12.560
Zeep even creates his own Microverse to generate
his planet’s electricity, which makes Rick’s

00:07:12.560 --> 00:07:16.569
Gooble Boxes obsolete, and his car battery unusable.

00:07:16.569 --> 00:07:20.599
Faced with a mirror image of themselves, you’d
expect a normal character to own up to their

00:07:20.599 --> 00:07:23.159
own hypocrisy and begin to change.

00:07:23.159 --> 00:07:24.509
But not Rick.

00:07:24.509 --> 00:07:29.110
Rick shamelessly parrots the same objections
Morty had made earlier to him – a shot which

00:07:29.110 --> 00:07:32.000
is almost visually identical to the earlier scene.

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:34.400
That just sounds like slavery with extra steps!

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:37.020
Ooh la la. Someones gonna get laid in college.

00:07:37.020 --> 00:07:40.020
Well, that just sounds like slavery with extra steps!

00:07:40.020 --> 00:07:44.159
Eek. Barba Durkel. Somebody's 
gonna get laid in college.

00:07:44.159 --> 00:07:50.100
And when Morty later calls Rick out on his hypocrisy, Rick seizes upon this as a chance to stop Zeep.

00:07:50.169 --> 00:07:55.060
This is the “things must change” part,
a new conflict is introduced: Rick must search

00:07:55.060 --> 00:07:58.990
for a scientist in Zeep’s Microverse working
on his own Microverse.

00:07:58.990 --> 00:08:01.949
Somewhere on this planet, there’s
got to be an arrogant scientist prick on the

00:08:01.949 --> 00:08:06.100
verge of Microverse technology, which would
threaten to make Zeep’s Floobal Cranks obsolete,

00:08:06.100 --> 00:08:11.400
forcing Zeep to say Microverses are bad, at
which point he’ll realize what a hypocrite he's being.

00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:13.740
His people will go back to stomping on their
Gooble Boxes.”

00:08:13.740 --> 00:08:17.190
This sets us up for Step 5: The character
gets what they wanted.

00:08:17.190 --> 00:08:21.169
If you look at the diagram, you’ll notice
Step 5 is at the very bottom of the circle,

00:08:21.169 --> 00:08:24.710
opposite of Step 1, where the hero is most
comfortable.

00:08:24.710 --> 00:08:29.550
Here, the hero has hit rock-bottom and has
finally found what they were looking for,

00:08:29.550 --> 00:08:32.169
even if it wasn’t quite what they had expected.

00:08:32.169 --> 00:08:35.950
This is the first major turning point of the
story, where your character’s motivations

00:08:35.950 --> 00:08:37.339
begin to change.

00:08:37.339 --> 00:08:41.729
If you want a plot twist, Harmon says to twist
here and twist hard.

00:08:41.729 --> 00:08:46.930
Rick reaches Step 5 when he gets what he wants:
he finds a scientist named Kyle in Zeep’s Microverse

00:08:46.930 --> 00:08:49.090
who’s creating his own Microverse.

00:08:49.090 --> 00:08:53.060
In an Inception-like moment, we travel into
a Microverse within a Microverse...

00:08:53.060 --> 00:08:54.780
Within another Microverse.

00:08:54.780 --> 00:09:00.810
Rick sits back and watches in smug self-satisfaction
as Zeep repeats to Kyle the same slavery rant

00:09:00.810 --> 00:09:03.540
we’ve heard third time in the last eight minutes.

00:09:03.540 --> 00:09:05.520
Did we mention that comedy comes in threes?

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:10.500
That just sounds like slavery with extra steps.

00:09:10.500 --> 00:09:15.420
But as Zeep continues his rant, he realizes
that his own universe is a Microverse – and

00:09:15.420 --> 00:09:18.529
that his whole existence was brought into
being through Rick.

00:09:18.529 --> 00:09:22.750
In an existential rage, Zeep and Rick begin
beating the crap out of each other.

00:09:22.750 --> 00:09:39.000
Meanwhile, Kyle crumbles into himself with the realization that he’s in a nesting doll of universes.

00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:44.130
But Rick’s triumph is short lived, which
brings us to Step 6: Paying a hefty price

00:09:44.130 --> 00:09:46.510
and suffering the consequences.

00:09:46.510 --> 00:09:49.760
Given that this model is circular, you can
imagine that there’s going to be a lot of

00:09:49.760 --> 00:09:51.450
symmetry going on here.

00:09:51.450 --> 00:09:56.300
Just as Step 2 is about preparing the hero
for their descent, Step 6 is about preparing

00:09:56.300 --> 00:09:57.850
them for their return.

00:09:57.850 --> 00:10:02.730
This step is all about the Hero testing their
newfound will against the unfeeling world,

00:10:02.730 --> 00:10:05.300
often getting their ass handed to them in the process.

00:10:05.300 --> 00:10:10.100
Rick gets exactly what he wanted: a Microverse
within Zeep’s Microverse.

00:10:10.100 --> 00:10:15.700
But in demonstrating Zeep’s hypocrisy, Rick
causes Kyle to have an existential meltdown

00:10:15.700 --> 00:10:19.600
and kill himself, trapping them all in the
Tinyverse.

00:10:19.600 --> 00:10:23.840
Unable to put aside their egos, Rick and Zeep
blame each other and set out to destroy

00:10:23.840 --> 00:10:26.630
one another instead of working together to escape.

00:10:26.630 --> 00:10:31.420
The two set up camp across from each other
and begin an all out war, completely forgetting

00:10:31.420 --> 00:10:33.000
about their current predicament.

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:37.600
Realizing that they’re going nowhere, Morty
decides he’s better off living with Tree People.

00:10:37.600 --> 00:10:42.310
But it’s actually Morty and his primitive
tribe of Tree People that forces Rick and Zeep

00:10:42.310 --> 00:10:48.700
to forge an alliance, which brings us to Step 7: 
Undoing the damage and returning the familiar world.

00:10:48.700 --> 00:10:53.980
Just as Step 4 is the final step before the
hero sinks to the bottom of the unfamiliar,

00:10:53.980 --> 00:10:57.410
Step 7 is the final step before they resurface.

00:10:57.410 --> 00:11:02.280
The only problem is that re-entering the old
world is just as difficult as leaving it.

00:11:02.280 --> 00:11:07.010
Once your hero tries to return, the denizens
of the deep are likely to give chase – and

00:11:07.010 --> 00:11:09.180
sometimes return with them.

00:11:09.180 --> 00:11:13.709
Using his control over the tree people, Morty
forces Rick and Zeep to put aside their egos

00:11:13.709 --> 00:11:16.810
and engineer a solution out of Kyle’s Tinyverse.

00:11:16.810 --> 00:11:23.000
But once they escaped it, all bets are off – and the two groups rush to exit the surrounding Microverse.

00:11:23.000 --> 00:11:27.449
On a metaphorical level, Zeep is the embodiment
of this lower world; he’s there to stop

00:11:27.449 --> 00:11:32.600
Rick from bringing the change back to the
original world - getting his car battery running again.

00:11:32.600 --> 00:11:37.089
On a more literal level, though, Zeep wants
to trap Rick in the Microverse because it’s

00:11:37.089 --> 00:11:40.920
the only way to keep his own Microverse from
being destroyed.

00:11:40.920 --> 00:11:46.310
In many ways, Zeep is Rick’s shadow, serving
as a dark reflection of all Rick’s undesirable

00:11:46.310 --> 00:11:48.050
traits and neuroses.

00:11:48.050 --> 00:11:52.250
Shadows are a common story device, and like
many shadow-figures before him, Zeep serves

00:11:52.250 --> 00:11:57.510
as a gatekeeper for this lower world – he
is the final obstacle a hero must face.

00:11:57.510 --> 00:12:01.820
Whether it’s Hans Gruber putting a gun to
John McClain’s wife in Die Hard, or Luke

00:12:01.820 --> 00:12:06.649
facing off against Darth Vader in Star Wars,
shadow characters offer a dark glimpse into

00:12:06.649 --> 00:12:10.490
what the hero could have been if they didn’t
undergo a transformation.

00:12:10.490 --> 00:12:15.120
The beauty of Rick &amp; Morty is that Rick is
completely aware of all his shortcomings and

00:12:15.120 --> 00:12:17.310
has no desire to fix them.

00:12:17.310 --> 00:12:21.490
The whole episode presents us with a series
of parallels between Rick and Zeep, making

00:12:21.490 --> 00:12:23.529
each look as bad as the other.

00:12:23.529 --> 00:12:27.730
When Rick tells Morty to run, it’s hard
to tell if he’s describing himself or Zeep:

00:12:27.730 --> 00:12:28.730
Run, Morty!

00:12:28.730 --> 00:12:31.600
That asshole’s willing to risk everything
he cares about just so to defeat me!

00:12:31.600 --> 00:12:32.600
He’s psychotic.

00:12:32.600 --> 00:12:36.579
As a result, when Rick and Zeep finally face
off in the episode’s conclusion, it’s

00:12:36.579 --> 00:12:41.089
not Rick battling the physical manifestations
of his demons in attempt to change.

00:12:41.089 --> 00:12:42.740
It’s just Rick fighting Rick.

00:12:42.740 --> 00:12:45.860
Even Morty feels the fight is unnecessary at this point.

00:12:45.860 --> 00:12:46.620
Don't do it.

00:12:46.620 --> 00:12:49.589
You quit school, but you still got some learnin to do.

00:12:49.589 --> 00:12:53.970
With Zeep taken care of, Rick and Morty finally
exit the Microverse, arriving back in their

00:12:53.970 --> 00:12:55.720
Ball Fondlers reality.

00:12:55.720 --> 00:12:59.730
This brings us to Step Eight: Change or the
futility of it.

00:12:59.730 --> 00:13:04.380
Normally, this is where the hero finally reaches
fulfillment, where he integrates all of the skills

00:13:04.380 --> 00:13:07.060
he learned on his journey into his old life.

00:13:07.060 --> 00:13:10.089
In The Karate Kid, Daniel can now Kung all
the Fu.

00:13:10.089 --> 00:13:13.540
In Saw II, Amanda has become the next Jigsaw
Killer.

00:13:13.540 --> 00:13:16.290
But remember, TV isn’t about change.

00:13:16.290 --> 00:13:18.420
The hero can never be fulfilled.

00:13:18.420 --> 00:13:23.000
Otherwise they’ll never desire another adventure,
and there will be no story to tune into.

00:13:23.000 --> 00:13:27.500
Once the two return to their Ball Fondlers
universe, Rick turns the key and the ignition starts.

00:13:27.500 --> 00:13:32.490
He explains to a confused Morty that Zeep
had either two choices: go back to Gooble

00:13:32.490 --> 00:13:36.680
Box technology or have Rick throw away a broken
Microverse battery.

00:13:36.680 --> 00:13:40.000
Peace among worlds, Rick.

00:13:40.000 --> 00:13:44.970
Fully embracing the futility of change, the
episode ends with the three sitting down for

00:13:44.970 --> 00:13:49.200
ice-cream, a dazed Morty and Summer shellshocked by the normalcy of the situation

00:13:49.200 --> 00:13:51.160
after all they’ve been through.

00:13:51.160 --> 00:13:55.670
In the end, Rick &amp; Morty is both immediately
familiar and radically different from anything

00:13:55.670 --> 00:13:56.949
we’ve seen on television.

00:13:56.949 --> 00:14:01.319
While the show regularly mocks popular culture
and continually plays off the audience’s

00:14:01.319 --> 00:14:06.019
expectations, at its core, the series is structured
just like any other TV show.

00:14:06.019 --> 00:14:09.149
But this is where the Rick &amp; Morty shines
the brightest.

00:14:09.149 --> 00:14:13.319
Because while shows like Happy Days and Leave
It To Beaver are content to leave us right

00:14:13.319 --> 00:14:17.889
where each episode started, Rick &amp; Morty uses
its circular structure to make us confront

00:14:17.889 --> 00:14:21.149
the depressing, crippling futility of change.

00:14:21.149 --> 00:14:26.339
Rick successfully re-enslaves a whole planet
to power his car battery, all so they can

00:14:26.339 --> 00:14:27.589
get some ice-cream.

00:14:27.589 --> 00:14:32.639
So while Rick Sanchez may not believe in much,
the creative team definitely believes in the

00:14:32.639 --> 00:14:35.000
power story structure.

